     "derivation": "Arabic قَزّ qazz 'raw silk, floss silk' (Nourai transliterates it qaz, without the doubled final consonant) is the Arabicised form of Persian kaž: Persian k- regularly answers Arabic q- in borrowings that entered before the Arabic script had a letter for /g/ or /ž/, and the Persian short a is kept. All three of Nourai's traditions say so: Lokotsch no. 1147 (printed p. 91) 'Ar. kazz: Rohseide, Flockseide [aus pers. kāz, gāz]'; Asbaghi p. 218 'qazz: \"rohe Seide\" pers. kaž, mp. kač'; and Mo'in's footnote to Borhan p.1637 'قز (معرب)', i.e. qaz is the mu'arrab (Arabicised) form, with the body of the Borhan article adding 'بعضی گفته‌اند که قز معرب کژ است'. The derived Arabic occupational noun qazzāz 'silk-rearer, silk-merchant' (Asbaghi p. 218, Pihan p. 176) shows the word fully naturalised. One caveat: Wiktionary treats Arabic قَزّ and خَزّ, Aramaic קָזָא qāzzā and Persian کژ as a set of merely 'akin' forms, so a route through Aramaic rather than direct Persian > Arabic borrowing cannot be excluded.",
