     "derivation": "Modern Persian گاز gāz 'surgical gauze, the light open-weave bandage cloth' is a twentieth-century loan straight from French gaze, taken over with the rest of the European medical vocabulary; French [gaz] is written گاز with the Persian letter gāf, which the Arabic script of قز could not supply. Mo'in tags it [ فر. ] — Faransavi, French — and gives exactly Nourai's gloss: 'گاز [ فر. ] (اِ.) ۱- تور نازک و لطیف که برای بستن زخم به کار می رود' (the second sense in Mo'in, the accelerator pedal, is the other French گاز, from gaz 'gas', a homonym). This closes the circle: the same Persian word that left the language as kaž re-enters it a third time, now through Arabic and French, as a hospital term.",
