     "derivation": "The word is real and the gloss is right — Ὠκεανός is in Homer the great fresh-water river that runs round the rim of the earth and the Titan who personifies it, and only later the outer sea — but the morphological analysis drawn here is not accepted today. Nourai's cut ō- + keanos, 'lying around', with ō- the preverb answering Sanskrit/Avestan ā and the second element from PIE *ḱey- 'to lie' (compare κεῖμαι), is the Benfey-Brugmann etymology built on the Vedic ā-śáyāna- 'lying upon (the waters)'. Kent p. 164 records it, but Walde-Pokorny I 358 f. states that Brückner and Brugmann-Thumb abandoned it 'wohl mit Recht', Beekes reconstructs a Pre-Greek *ūkʲān- and argues that a circumambient world-river is not an Indo-European idea, and Klein calls the Greek word 'of uncertain etymology'; Michael Janda alone has revived the comparison, positing *ō-ḱey-m̥[h₁]no-. The variant the node's NOTE reports from FSD:1707 and TAD:5, deriving it from ὠκύς 'swift' (as though the ocean were 'the swift one'), is a folk-etymological guess of the older Arabic and popular lexicography and is entertained by no modern handbook. Verdict: the word yes, this derivation no.",
