     "derivation": "Middle Persian kač 'raw/floss silk' is the immediate ancestor of the New Persian word and is recorded by both of Nourai's references: Mo'in's footnote to Borhan-e Qate' p.1637 gives 'پهلوی kac' (citing Tavadia), and Asbaghi p.218 writes 'pers. kaž, mp. kač' (citing Farahvashi). It is the word for the coarse, unreeled silk left over after the fine thread has been drawn off the cocoon — precisely 'inexpensive silk'. The Pahlavi final affricate -č is continued in New Persian both unchanged (کج kaj) and voiced (کژ kaž), with a further de-affricated by-form کز kaz and a dialectal غژ ġaž; Borhan-e Qate' registers all of them on the same page. Wiktionary's Persian article s.v. کژ likewise begins 'From Middle Persian kač'.",
